MOUNT(1)                     System Administration                    MOUNT(1)

NAME
       mount - mount file systems, or list the mounted ones

SYNOPSIS
       mount [-l] [-t types]
       mount -a [-fnv] [-t types] [-O options]
       mount [-fnrvw] [-t type] [-o options] source directory
       mount [-fnrvw] [-o options] source|directory
       mount -o remount[,options] directory

DESCRIPTION
       mount  attaches  the  file system on a device (or one that needs no de-
       vice, such as tmpfs) to a directory, the mount point, so that its files
       appear there.  umount(1) detaches it again.

       Without arguments mount lists the mounted file systems, one line each,

              source on directory type type (options)

       from /proc/mounts; -t limits the list to some types.

       With one argument, a mount point or a source, the  line  of  /etc/fstab
       for  it  is  mounted,  with that line's type and options (options given
       with -o are added to them).  mount -a mounts every line  of  /etc/fstab
       that  is  not marked noauto, is not swap and is not mounted already, in
       the order of the file -- what init(8) does at start-up.

       Mounting needs root; listing does not.

   Sources
       /dev/device
              A block device: /dev/sda1, /dev/nvme0n1p2, /dev/mmcblk0p1.

       UUID=uuid
              The device whose file system has this UUID (blkid(8) shows it).

       LABEL=label
              The device whose file system has this label.

       PARTUUID=uuid
              The partition with this unique partition GUID (GPT) or MBR iden-
              tifier.

       PARTLABEL=name
              The partition with this name (GPT).

       word   A file system without a device: tmpfs.

       The tags are looked up in /dev/disk/by-uuid, by-label, by-partuuid  and
       by-partlabel.   Bind  mounts and loop devices (a directory or a file as
       the source) are not offered.

   File system types
       ext4   The LikeOS root file system and data disks (also ext2  and  ext3
              file systems, read and written as ext4).

       vfat   FAT12, FAT16 and FAT32: the EFI system partition, USB sticks and
              memory cards (fat, fat32 and msdos are taken for the same).

       tmpfs  A  file  system  in  memory,  empty  when mounted, gone when un-
              mounted.

       Without -t the type is the one /etc/fstab gives,  or  else  the  kernel
       recognises the file system on the device.

OPTIONS
       -a, --all
              Mount every file system of /etc/fstab that is not noauto.

       -t, --types list
              The  file  system  type.  With -a or a listing: the types to in-
              clude,  separated  by  commas;  a  no  prefix  excludes  a  type
              (notmpfs),  and  a  list  starting  with  no excludes all of its
              types.

       -O, --test-opts list
              With -a: only the lines that have these options (no  option  se-
              lects the lines without it).

       -o, --options list
              Mount options, separated by commas (below).

       -r, -R, --read-only
              Mount read-only, the same as -o ro.

       -w, --rw, --read-write
              Mount  read-write,  the  same as -o rw.  A device that is write-
              protected is mounted read-only when neither rw nor -w was  asked
              for.

       -f, --fake
              Do  everything but the mount itself (with -v: show what would be
              mounted).

       -n, --no-mtab
              Accepted; there is no /etc/mtab to update.

       -v, --verbose
              Say what is mounted, with which options.

       -l, --show-labels
              Accepted with a listing.

       -h, --help
              Show a summary of the options.

       -V, --version
              Show the version.

MOUNT OPTIONS
       The options every file system takes:

       ro, rw Read-only, or read-write (the default).

       defaults
              rw, suid, dev, exec, auto, async.

       nosuid, suid
              Ignore (or honour) the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits of pro-
              grams.

       nodev, dev
              Do not (or do) give access to device files on the file system.

       noexec, exec
              Do not (or do) allow programs on the file system to be run.

       noatime, atime, relatime, strictatime, nodiratime, diratime
              When the access times of files (and directories) are updated.

       sync, async, dirsync
              Write data (or directory changes) at once, or when convenient.

       lazytime
              Keep time stamp updates in memory longer.

       nosymfollow
              Do not follow symbolic links on the file system.

       remount
              Change the options of a file system that is mounted already: its
              flags (ro and rw among them) are those it has,  changed  by  the
              options given.

       noauto, auto
              In  /etc/fstab:  not  mounted  (or  mounted)  by mount -a and at
              start-up.

       nofail In /etc/fstab: a device that is not there is no error.

       X-mount.mkdir[=mode]
              Make the mount point when it does not exist (with  mode,  octal;
              default 0755).

       Anything  else  is  the  file  system's own option and passed on to it.
       ext4 takes norecovery (or noload: mount read-only without replaying the
       journal) and recovery=ask (a journal that needs replaying fails the
       mount with "Structure needs cleaning" instead of being replayed; a
       mount without the option replays it).  ntfs takes recover (replay an
       unclean journal, which is otherwise left alone and the volume mounted
       read-only) and recovery=ask (an unclean journal that recover would
       replay fails the mount the same way).  vfat takes uid=n and gid=n (the owner every file shows;
       FAT stores none), umask=octal, fmask=octal and dmask=octal (the permis-
       sion bits taken away from every file, from files, from directories; the
       defaults  give  files  0644   and   directories   0755),   and   short-
       name=lower|win95|winnt|mixed (how names that fit 8.3 are shown and cre-
       ated).   tmpfs  takes size=n (bytes, with a k, m or g suffix, or a per-
       centage of the memory with %; default: a quarter of the memory), nr_in-
       odes=n (the most files), mode=octal (of  the  root  directory;  default
       1777), uid=n and gid=n (the owner of the root directory).

EXIT STATUS
       0      Success.

       1      Wrong usage, or no permission.

       2      A system error.

       32     The mount failed.

       64     Some of several mounts failed (-a).

FILES
       /etc/fstab
              The file systems to mount, see fstab(5).

       /proc/mounts
              What is mounted now.

       /dev/disk/by-uuid, by-label, by-partuuid, by-partlabel
              Where the tags are looked up.

EXAMPLES
       mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt
       mount -t vfat -o uid=1000,gid=1000 LABEL=STICK /media/stick
       mount -t tmpfs -o size=512m,mode=1777 tmpfs /scratch
       mount -o remount,ro /
       mount -a -t notmpfs

SEE ALSO
       umount(1), fstab(5), blkid(8), lsblk(8), findfs(8)

